LookupCache: Take the range off the overcommit list before freeing it.

A thread's lookup cache is reserved without being committed, and its range is
put on the frontend's list of ranges to commit at the first access
(MarkOvercommitRange). The destructor freed the memory first and took the
range off the list afterwards.

Between the two, a thread that is starting can be given the same address for
its own cache (Wine allocates top down, so the address just freed is the next
one given out) and mark the same range, which the thread that is ending then
unmarks. The first lookup of the new thread is an access violation that the
overcommit tracker does not know, nobody handles it, and the thread never
reaches its first instruction.

With 50 threads at a time, each ending at once, about one thread in 1,500 was
lost this way under Wine on macOS (macos/tests/sync_test.c, "threads that
start while others end").

